# Recipes

<p class="lede">
Each recipe is a whole plugin, verified on a dev daemon with the commands on its page. The code shown is the code
that is checked: pages read it from the repository at build time.
</p>

| Recipe | What it shows | Uses |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Timeline renderer](/docs/recipes/timeline-renderer) | A card under every agent turn that touched files, and a screen listing all turns. | `server.on("agent.turn_ended")`, `timeline.append`, `addTimelineRenderer`, `addScreen`, an RPC |
| [Settings screen](/docs/recipes/settings) | A settings screen whose values the server reads when a turn ends. | `defineSettings`, `registerSettings`, `useSettings`, `addSettingsScreen`, `addCommandCenterItem` |
| [Server hooks](/docs/recipes/server-hooks) | An MCP server injected into every new agent, and a log line for every finished turn. | `server.before("agent.create")`, `server.on("agent.turn_ended")` |

The timeline renderer is the `agent-hook` template of `ppd create`, which the devkit's own tests create, check and
run. The other two live in
[`docs-site/recipes`](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/tree/main/docs-site/recipes), and CI runs
`ppd check` on them.

<h2 id="try">Try one</h2>

From any plugin repo with `paseo-plugin-devkit` installed and a dev daemon running:

```sh
npx ppd check path/to/recipes/settings
npx ppd install path/to/recipes/settings
```

A recipe's devDependencies are there for types and tests only: Paseo provides `@getpaseo/plugin`, `react`,
`react-native` and `zod` at runtime, so a recipe installs without `npm install`.
